errcases: the suite of the error messages
=========================================

What it pins: the text of the diagnostics of `patsopt --typecheck -d` for about 40 small
programs that fail to typecheck, one per message family (type mismatch, sorts, linear
variables, constraints with `where`/`assuming`, termination metrics, views, selections,
fold on demand with its hints, the aliases of the printed types, trans2 errors, the no-GC
rejection, a warning, a range of several lines, tabs in the echoed line) and one case
(`legacy.dats`) run with `--legacy-errors` to pin the old form. Each program has a comment
line that says what it tests.

Run it:

    make -C src/test errtest          # also part of `make -C src test`
    sh src/test/errcases/run.sh       # the same; `run.sh NAME...` runs some cases

The runner prints `errcases: N cases, M failed` and exits non-zero on failure. It needs only
src/patsopt (override with PATSOPT=...). It runs `patsopt --typecheck -d NAME.dats` from this
directory with LC_ALL=C and PATSOPT_LEGACY_ERRORS unset (src/test/Makefile exports it for the
md5 suites, that is why this suite has its own Makefile and is started with `$(MAKE) -C`),
stdout and stderr together, and appends the exit status as a last line `exit N`.
patsopt prints the absolute path of the file even for a relative argument, so the runner
removes the directory of the suite from the output.

Update the expected files after a change of the messages:

    sh src/test/errcases/run.sh update          # or NAME...

then REVIEW every changed file in expected/ by hand (`git diff`): these files are the record
of what the user sees, they are not recorded from another implementation. Check that the text
is correct and readable: no raw S2E.../S2RT... text, no $stamp suffixes, no (-1), the source
echo is present and under the right message, wording is plain. Known defects that the expected
files still show are the ones to fix in the compiler, then update them.

A new case: add NAME.dats (under 15 lines, first line a comment) and run `run.sh update NAME`.
